  8230	while an effect boundary still holds prior authorization.
  8231	
  8232	Revocation is an explicit local atomic transition to revoked, increments the
  8233	generation, forbids all future admissions of that key and closes old streams.
  8234	Removing an allowlisted host also increments generation and invalidates streams.
  8235	Success means the durable decision committed; the documented one-second close
  8236	bound does not permit any later dispatch or mutation from the stale generation.
  8237	A crash after commit but before acknowledgements cannot restore authority.
  8238	Failed commits report failure; they cannot be reported as absence or success.
  8239	Revocation is local: the operator must distribute it explicitly to every peer;
  8240	a disconnected peer with stale trust cannot be claimed globally revoked.
  8241	Loss/copy/compromise of a key requires revocation and new out-of-band enrollment,
  8242	not same-key recovery. Host UUID reuse after total trust-state loss requires
  8243	operator reconciliation of every peer, never automatic enrollment.
