   522	
   523	## Safe Process and Filesystem Primitives
   524	
   525	[`internal/secprim`](internal/secprim) implements the shared safe
   526	process and filesystem primitives of Sections 16.3, 16.4, and 16.7:
   527	no-follow path handling, staging containment, structured argv,
   528	environment allowlists, terminal control-string escaping, and secret
   529	redaction for free-text streams. It is a library behind the production
   530	launch and rendering paths, not a second authority: path grammars stay
   531	in [`internal/scalar`](internal/scalar) (the member gate delegates every
   532	shared rule there and adds only encoded dots, overlong separators, and
   533	the Windows alternate-stream, trailing-dot/space, and device rules the
   534	section names), byte-counted SpawnPlan rules stay in
   535	[`internal/provhost`](internal/provhost) (which delegates its
   536	environment-name grammar here so the two cannot drift), Structured
   537	Error detail redaction stays in [`internal/axerror`](internal/axerror),
   538	SSH argv admission stays in [`internal/config`](internal/config), and
   539	the `ax pane SESSION_ID` entrypoint rule stays in
   540	[`internal/terminalbackend`](internal/terminalbackend). Trust for
   541	external executables is deliberately not implemented here:
   542	`provider.trustCandidate` (Section 7.1, with the approving owner fact)
   543	and `terminalbackend.DigestFile` (Section 4.B trust tuple, with no owner
   544	fact) differ by contract, and a third path would be another copy, not
   545	convergence.
   546	
   547	`Guard.Resolve` maps one staging member to its lexical commit path under
   548	the member grammar plus an independent joined-prefix check (the prefix
   549	conjunct is unreachable through the entry and stays as defense in
   550	depth, pinned by a generated-corpus tripwire), an optional managed set,
   551	and Unicode case-fold collision detection. Lexical resolution alone
   552	does not contain: a symlinked intermediate directory redirects any
   553	path-string open outside the root, because `O_NOFOLLOW` constrains only
   554	the final component. The commit half is `Guard.Open`, which first binds
   555	the passed handle to the guard root (fstat device/inode compare,
   556	refusing a foreign directory handle as `guard root mismatch`
   557	identically on both platforms) and then walks every component relative
   558	to the verified `OpenNoFollowDir` root handle (`openat` with
   559	`O_NOFOLLOW` per component on unix, never re-resolving a path string;
   560	a best-effort Lstat component walk from the verified root path on
   561	Windows) and refuses a symlinked intermediate at any depth at commit
   562	time as `member symlink escape`. `OpenNoFollowFile`/`OpenNoFollowDir`
   563	open without following a trailing symlink (`O_NOFOLLOW` on unix; an
   564	Lstat gate refusing symlinks and irregular reparse points with a
   565	stated check-then-open TOCTOU bound on Windows) and re-verify shape on
   566	the descriptor; the unix open sets `O_NONBLOCK` so a FIFO refuses
   567	instead of stalling the opener. Resolve-then-`OpenNoFollowFile` is
   568	not a contained composition (documented on the function); commit
   569	through `Guard.Open`. Wired today are the argv, environment, escaping,
   570	and redaction surfaces below; the path/containment half has no
   571	production caller yet and is proven through its own suite.
   572	`Command` builds only from argv arrays — the package has no shell
   573	constructor, proven by a token scan plus a behavioral no-`os/exec`
   574	import gate that catches the token-preserving mutant.
   575	`BuildEnv` builds the exact child environment from an allowlist plus
   576	disjoint literals (absent names skipped, never defaulted), wired into
   577	`provhost.ExecRunner.Env`: nil inherits, an empty list denies all —
   578	the two are tested as distinct obligations. `EscapeForTerminal`
   579	neutralizes ANSI/OSC, C0/C1 controls, DEL, bidi overrides and isolates,
   580	directional marks, zero-width characters, and invalid UTF-8 while
   581	keeping newline, tab, and legitimate text (idempotent, so double-wiring
   582	is safe), wired into all six `cliresult` text paths (`Log`, `Progress`,
   583	interactive `Prompt`, the non-interactive refusal text, and both `Emit`
   584	directions, each pinned by a hostile-content test) with JSON mode left
   585	byte-exact. `Redact` scrubs caller-known secrets, private-key blocks,
   586	sensitive `key=value` pairs, and URL userinfo; Section 16.2 claims no
   587	reliable content-level scrubbing and neither does this package — the
   588	bound is stated on the function. The secret-site census derives every
   589	secret-named declaration, parameter, tag, and literal across the
   590	secret-handling packages from syntax and requires a disposition row
   591	with a verified witness for each (names outside the 13-token
   592	vocabulary are outside the witness, stated as a bound), the Section
   593	16.2 class roster derives all nine exclusion classes from the pinned
   594	document the same way, and the five archive-`nopath` rows claim only
   595	the bound the recursive import scan proves (no archive-writer import
   596	anywhere under `internal/`, nested packages included — not the absence
   597	of every non-archive emission shape). The refusal inventory derives all
   598	42 constructor call sites and additionally fails on a refusal built
   599	outside the constructors when spelled with the `Error` identifier
   600	(constructor aliases, `Error` literals, `new(Error)`, `*Error`
   601	factories, value escapes); a refusal type spelled through a type alias
   602	or defined type is outside the witness, stated as a bound in
   603	`errors.go` and pinned by `TestRefusalInventorySpellingBoundIsStated`.
   604	Windows behavior is compile-and-vet only, never executed; the Lstat
   605	reparse mapping is established from the go1.25.5 runtime source
   606	(`src/os/types_windows.go`): `ModeSymlink` for true symlinks and
   607	`ModeIrregular` for the default arm (junctions, mount points,
   608	AppExecLinks, cloud placeholders) are both refused, with two stated
   609	counterexamples to any universal claim — `AF_UNIX` surfaces as
   610	`ModeSocket` (refused later by the regular-target gate) and `DEDUP`
   611	carries no type bit (regular by design), neither escape-capable. The
   612	package mutates no durable state and keeps no cache, so it has no
   613	crash or idempotency surface.
